findings.body:
The diff for .topic-meta-data h5 a reads:
-      color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%);
+      color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 30%));

The $lightness argument passed to the $primary branch was changed from 30% to 70%. The PR's stated purpose is to add a $secondary fallback for dark themes without altering the light-theme appearance, but this line now resolves to a much lighter color in light themes. The mobile/topic-post.scss counterpart (line 182 in mobile/topic-post.scss) keeps the original lightness; this case is the only one in the PR where the $primary lightness was accidentally mutated.

The diff reads:
-        color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%);
+        color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%));

The $primary lightness was bumped from 30% to 50%, so in light themes the username link now renders noticeably lighter than before. The mobile/user.scss change for the same selector has the identical mistake; the desktop/topic-post.scss .arrow/.highlight-action etc. all keep their original primary lightness, so this looks like an accidental edit rather than an intentional color tweak.

Diff:
-    color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 20%);
+    color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%));

The $primary lightness jumped from 20% to 50%, so the topic-map h3 heading now appears much lighter in light themes than it did on master. Compare with desktop/topic-post.scss line 321, where the equivalent .topic-map h3 was correctly converted keeping the original 20% lightness. This divergence means desktop and mobile render the topic-map title in different shades of $primary, and the mobile light theme visually regresses.

Diff:
-  color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%);
+  color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 70%));

The $primary lightness was dropped from 70% to 30%, so the flag-modal custom-message-length counter text now appears much darker in light themes than before. It is also inconsistent with the equivalent selector in desktop/modal.scss (line 94), which correctly keeps the original 70% primary lightness. The two stylesheets should produce the same color in the light theme for parity between desktop and mobile flagging modals.
